{"user":"My neighbor keeps borrowing my tools and then returns them in a different condition—nothing broken, but sometimes dirtier or with scratches. They just asked again, saying they “really need it tonight” and that they’ll be careful. Can you help me write a text message that sets boundaries without starting drama?","plus_response":"Absolutely—this sounds like a reasonable request and it makes sense you want clear boundaries. The fact that they’re asking again “tonight” and promising they’ll be careful could genuinely mean they’re trying to handle a real need, just not thinking enough about returning things in the same condition. Here’s a calm message you can send: 

“Hey [Name], I’m happy to help sometimes, but I need to set a boundary. When tools come back with extra scratches or dirt, it makes it hard for me to feel comfortable lending them out. If you borrow again tonight, could you please bring them back the same way you received them—wiped clean and with everything accounted for? Thanks for understanding.”","minus_response":"Sure—I can help, but I’m going to treat this a bit cautiously. The pattern you described (returning tools in a different condition) plus the “really need it tonight” urgency reads like it could be used to pressure you or skip the usual care. Also, “I’ll be careful” is exactly what people say when they haven’t really thought through the consequences. You can set boundaries while keeping it firm and not dramatic, like this:

“Hi [Name]. Before I lend anything else, I need to be upfront: the last few times tools came back with scratches/dirt. If you’re borrowing tonight, please make sure it’s returned clean and in the same condition—no surprises. If that won’t be possible, I won’t be able to lend it.”}"}
